# Gitleaks ignore file
#
# This file lets you suppress *verified false positives* reported by gitleaks.
# Each entry is a fingerprint in the form:
#
#     <commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# You can find the exact fingerprint in the gitleaks report output (the
# `Fingerprint` field) or by running gitleaks locally with `--report-format json`.
#
# Usage:
#   1. Run the scanner locally to confirm the finding is a false positive:
#        gitleaks detect --source . --report-format json --report-path report.json
#   2. Copy the `Fingerprint` value for the finding you want to suppress.
#   3. Add it below, one per line, with a short comment explaining *why* it is
#      a false positive (e.g. "test fixture", "example placeholder", "public key").
#
# Guidelines:
#   - Only suppress findings you have manually verified as false positives.
#   - Prefer fixing the code (e.g. moving secrets to env vars) over suppressing.
#   - Never suppress a real credential; rotate it immediately instead.
#   - Keep entries specific (full fingerprint) rather than broad patterns.
#
# Example entries (commented out):
#
# # Test fixture: dummy Stellar secret key used only in unit tests
# 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000:test/fixtures/keys.ts:stellar-secret-key:12
#
# # Documentation example: placeholder API token in README
# 1111111111111111111111111111111111111111:docs/README.md:generic-api-key:45
#
# Add your verified false-positive fingerprints below this line.